WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:12.390
In this video, you give live migration traffic its own network in SUSE Virtualization, so moving virtual machines does not compete with other management traffic.

00:00:12.390 --> 00:00:21.020
Under Advanced, Settings, find vm-migration-network. It has no value, so migrations share the management network.

00:00:21.020 --> 00:00:23.870
Open its actions menu,

00:00:23.870 --> 00:00:26.670
and select Edit Setting.

00:00:26.670 --> 00:00:29.240
Select Enabled.

00:00:29.240 --> 00:00:38.330
Choose the cluster network that carries the traffic. These nodes have one network interface, so pick mgmt.

00:00:38.330 --> 00:00:45.170
Enter a VLAN ID that the physical switch carries between all nodes, here nine nine nine.

00:00:45.170 --> 00:00:51.770
Enter an unused IP range. Each node gets an address from it for migration traffic.

00:00:51.770 --> 00:00:56.010
Optionally, exclude addresses that are already in use,

00:00:56.010 --> 00:00:58.460
such as a gateway address.

00:00:58.460 --> 00:01:00.890
Select Save.

00:01:00.890 --> 00:01:06.330
The setting is now marked Modified. Select Show to see its value.

00:01:06.330 --> 00:01:16.510
The setting now shows the cluster network, VLAN, and IP range. New live migrations use this dedicated network.

00:01:16.510 --> 00:01:24.128
Live migrations now use the dedicated network. Next, you live-migrate a virtual machine.
